The long-run average cost (LRAC) curve represents the lowest possible cost for each output level when all inputs are variable. The short-run average cost (SRAC) curve assumes at least one input is fixed. For any given output, the firm can choose an optimal plant size in the long run that minimizes costs. Therefore, the LRAC curve must lie below or touch the SRAC curves; it cannot be above them.

The LRAC curve is formed by the envelope of all possible SRAC curves. Each SRAC curve touches the LRAC curve at its minimum point. At this tangency point, the firm is operating efficiently for that specific plant size in both the short and long run. Thus, the LRAC always intersects each SRAC curve at the minimum of the SRAC curve.

